WEBVTT

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.690
Here’s how to run “Draw your team’s loop”.

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:10.796
It takes about forty minutes, ideally at a whiteboard or on the big canvas.

00:00:10.846 --> 00:00:15.981
Draw a loop: plan, then make, then review, then back to plan.

00:00:16.031 --> 00:00:22.101
Explain it in one sentence: every team goes through this loop, whether they plan it or not.

00:00:22.151 --> 00:00:27.788
Now ask everyone to write the meetings and habits the team already has, one per post-it.

00:00:27.838 --> 00:00:33.705
Stand-ups, weekly meetings, one-on-ones, reviews, demos, the Friday chat.

00:00:33.755 --> 00:00:39.484
Then place each one on the loop where it belongs. Is it planning? Making? Reviewing?

00:00:39.534 --> 00:00:41.581
This is where it gets interesting.

00:00:41.631 --> 00:00:48.514
Usually you’ll see a crowd of post-its around planning, a few around making, and almost nothing around reviewing.

00:00:48.564 --> 00:00:50.922
Ask the team: “What do you notice?”

00:00:50.972 --> 00:00:57.890
Now go through the post-its one by one, and mark each one: keep, change, combine, or remove. Move quickly.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:03.012
Don’t debate every meeting in depth; you can come back to the difficult ones.

00:01:03.062 --> 00:01:08.707
Then, for each of the three moments, agree when and how it happens, and write it above the loop.

00:01:08.757 --> 00:01:13.117
For example: “We plan every second Monday, from one shared list.”

00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:15.708
“We make in the mornings, without meetings.”

00:01:15.758 --> 00:01:19.163
“We review every other Thursday, with real examples.”

00:01:19.213 --> 00:01:25.963
Finally, agree to try the new rhythm for one full cycle, maybe two or four weeks, and then look at it again.

00:01:26.013 --> 00:01:28.572
Frame it as an experiment, not a law.

00:01:28.622 --> 00:01:32.689
It makes people more willing to try, and it gives you a natural moment to adjust.

